%[text] # **Quick Model and Eigen Visualization**
%[text] A compact model is used to introduce undeformed geometry, deformed shape, and mode-shape plots. The scale factors affect only visualization and must not be interpreted as changes to the analysis result.
%[text] At any point during model creation, you can run the `ops.vis.plotModel()` or `ops.vis.plotEigen()` to visualize the current model’s geometric details and eigen modes.
%[text] ### Model visualization
clc; clear; close all;
opsMAT = OpenSeesMatlab();
ops = opsMAT.opensees;
%[text] For example, the `tool` property provides a function `loadExamples` to run some built-in models. Of course, you can run your own model; the built-in model is used here for demonstration purposes only.
ops.wipe();
opsMAT.utils.loadExamples("ArchBridge2");
% or your model here
%[text] We can visualize the model using the `plotModel` function in the `vis` attribute.
opsMAT.vis.plotModel(); %[output:10ad11ca] %[output:77e8b577]
%[text] We can retrieve data from the current model, which returns a nested `struct`. You can view the data using MATLAB's workspace variables.
modelData = opsMAT.post.getModelData();
disp(modelData) %[output:0dfe37f1]
%[text] Finally, we can customize the control parameters for model visualization. Let's first take a look at the default parameter settings.
opts = opsMAT.vis.defaultPlotModelOptions;
% disp(opts)
%[text] Then
opts.nodes.show = true;
opts.nodes.size = 20;
opts.nodes.showLabels = true;

opsMAT.vis.plotModel(opts=opts); %[output:338adb45] %[output:9d452364]
axis off %[output:9d452364]
%[text] #### Visualization based on Matlab GUI
opsMAT.vis.plotModelGUI();
%[text] ![](text:image:6d86)
%[text] #### Visualization based on Polyscope GUI
opsMAT.vis.polyscope.plotModel();
%[text] ![](text:image:4117)
%[text] ### Eigen visualization
%[text] First, we need to save the eigenvalue analysis results data for future reuse.
%[text] Then, data is retrieved from the file, returning a nested `structure` that stores the various results of the eigenvalue analysis.
tag = 1;
opsMAT.post.saveEigenData(tag, 10, solver='-genBandArpack');  % save %[output:612f43ba]
eigenData = opsMAT.post.getEigenData(odbTag=tag);  % get
disp(eigenData) %[output:63af3296]
%[text] Using this data, we can visualize the first modal shapes.
opsMAT.vis.plotEigen(1, eigenData); %[output:36369944]
%[text] and 5th
opsMAT.vis.plotEigen(5, eigenData); %[output:114abe08]
%[text] Similarly, we obtain the default parameters.
opts = opsMAT.vis.defaultPlotEigenOptions;
% disp(opts.help)
%[text] For example:
opts.color.useColormap = true;
% opts.color.colormap = jet(256);
modeTags = [1 3 5 7];
cmps = {"parula", "turbo", "cool", "winter"};

% Create a figure at position (100,100) with width=1200px and height=900px
figure('Position', [100, 100, 1200, 900]); %[output:26e06b8e]
for i = 1:4 %[output:group:45e87338]
    subplot(2,2,i) %[output:26e06b8e]
    ax = gca;
    opsMAT.vis.plotEigen(modeTags(i), eigenData, opts=opts, ax=ax);
    axis off;
    colormap(ax, cmps{i});
end %[output:group:45e87338]
%[text] #### Visualization based on Matlab GUI
opsMAT.vis.plotEigenGUI(eigenData);
%[text] ![](text:image:0cc6)
%[text] #### Visualization based on Polyscope GUI
opsMAT.vis.polyscope.plotEigen(eigenData);
%[text] ![](text:image:1032)

%[text] ### Plotting checks
%[text] Undeformed geometry should confirm connectivity and support locations. Deformed and modal plots use visual scale factors, so amplitudes must be read from response data rather than measured from the figure.

